window.PH_BOOK_LOAD({"id":"21-02","translated":true,"translator":"AI (Claude)","sections":[{"slug":"21-02-01","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-01/","title_he":"א – משמעות הבגד והציציות","title_en":"1. The Meaning of the Garment and the Tzitzit","body_en":"<p>At first, in the Garden of Eden, man did not need clothes, since there was no disgrace in his nakedness; but once he was drawn after the desires of the body and sinned, and evil penetrated into him, he began to be ashamed of his nakedness. As it is said (Bereshit 3:8-11): \"And the man and his wife hid from the presence of the Lord God among the trees of the garden. And the Lord God called to the man and said to him: Where are you? And he said: I heard Your voice in the garden, and I was afraid because I was naked, and I hid. And He said: Who told you that you were naked? Have you eaten from the tree of which I commanded you not to eat?\" Following his sin, man was driven out of the Garden of Eden and needed protection from cold, rain and the rays of the sun. And God had pity on him and made him clothes, as it is said (there 3:21): \"And the Lord God made for Adam and his wife garments of skin, and clothed them.\"</p>\n<p>The garment hides a person's flaws and thereby gives him dignity; but it may be a false dignity meant to deceive others by hiding evil intentions, and then the garment (beged) expresses betrayal (begida), and the cloak (me'il) treachery (me'ila). On the other hand, the garment may express a person's aspiration to the good and the beautiful, while disowning his evil tendencies and trying to make them disappear, and thereby give a person true dignity (see Resisei Layla 34). The most dignified garment is the tasseled tallit, for we have learned (above 1:2) that the square tallit expresses all the forces stored up in man and in the world, and the tzitzit the 613 mitzvot that guide a person how to bring them into actuality. And there is no garment more beautiful than this, as the Sages said (Shabbat 23b): \"One who is careful with tzitzit merits a beautiful tallit.\"</p>\n<p>The sages of the Kabbala explained that the tallit hints at the 'surrounding light', that is, at the high Divine illumination which, because of its height and greatness, a person cannot grasp, but it influences him (see Peninei Halakha, Sukkot 1:7), and out of its influence he understands well the 'inner light', that is, the defined ideas in the Torah and its mitzvot which the threads of the tzitzit express (as explained above 1:2). The Sages said (Bamidbar Rabba 25:21) that the tzitzit hint at the 613 mitzvot, for the numerical value of 'tzitzit' is six hundred, and with the five knots and eight threads in the gedil, 613.</p>\n<p>Only a garment that has four corners is obliged in tzitzit, because only such a garment expresses the world on all its sides (Or Ha-Ḥayim, Bamidbar 15:39). That is, only out of connection to the whole of reality that God created can one draw forth the 613 mitzvot of the Torah, which pave the repaired way to bringing the forces hidden in the whole of reality into actuality, in a way that adds blessing to man and the world. In the sense of 'derekh eretz preceded the Torah'.</p>\n<p>The tzitzit must be attached to the corner, which is the point at the edge of the garment. And the tzitzit itself is at first wound as a gedil, to express the general ideas in the guidance of the Torah, and from the gedil they divide into eight threads (below halakha 11). That is, out of the general ideas, the guidance of the Torah branches out gradually down to the details of the mitzvot and the laws.</p>\n<p>The Sages asked (Mishnat Rabbi Eliezer 14): From where did Israel merit to adorn their garments with tzitzit? And they answered: from Shem son of Noaḥ, for when Noaḥ became drunk and was disgraced, Shem went with Yefet his brother and they covered their father in such a way that he would not be disgraced (Bereshit 9:21-23), and God paid Shem's reward to his sons, \"for He gave them the mitzva of tzitzit, which is splendour and glory in this world and in the World to Come\". And Yefet, who was somewhat less meticulous in the mitzva, benefited his sons in that they would merit burial and their bodies would not be disgraced (Bereshit Rabba 36:6). And some say that Israel merited tzitzit in the merit of Avraham our father, who saved the people of Sedom from the four kings and could have received their property, but chose not to benefit from their property, \"from a thread to a shoelace\" (Bereshit 14:23), and in this merit his sons merited the great honour of the threads of the tzitzit (there, and Sota 17a).</p>","notes_en":[]},{"slug":"21-02-02","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-02/","title_he":"ב – ארבע כנפות","title_en":"2. Four Corners","body_en":"<p>The garment obliged in tzitzit is a garment that has four corners, as it is said (Devarim 22:12): \"You shall make for yourself twisted threads on the four corners of your covering with which you cover yourself.\" But a round or triangular garment is exempt from tzitzit (Menaḥot 43b; Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 10:1). The corner is a point with a right angle at the edge of the garment, and therefore a square garment one of whose corners was cut and made round becomes exempt from tzitzit, since it does not have four corners (Shulḥan Arukh 10:9). For the corner to become round, it must be recognisable to onlookers that it is round and not square. Some require for this that the rounding extend over about six cm on each side of the corner (Mor U-Ketzi'a there).</p>\n<p>A garment that has five corners or more is obliged in tzitzit, because four corners are included within five corners, and the extra corner does not cancel the four corners by which the garment is obliged in tzitzit. The tzitzit should be put on the four corners furthest from one another (Menaḥot 43b; Shulḥan Arukh 10:1; Mishna Berura 2).<sup>1</sup></p>\n<p>One who wishes to turn an undershirt or shirt into a garment obliged in tzitzit must cut most of the garment on both its sides, so that two corners are formed in front and two behind, as in a 'tallit katan'. The garment is measured from the upper side on the shoulders down to its end: if most of it is open, this is a garment of four corners obliged in tzitzit. And if most of it is closed, and the sleeves too are counted as part of the closed portion, it is not considered a garment of four corners and is exempt from tzitzit (Shulḥan Arukh 10:7; Mishna Berura 25).<sup>2</sup></p>\n<p>A shirt or a suit with a folded collar, although the collar forms a kind of four corners on it, is exempt from tzitzit. For as a rule, a garment obliged in tzitzit is one that has two corners in front and two behind, opposite each other, whereas in these garments there are four corners in front (Rema 10:12). One can further explain that it is the nature of a garment that its corners hang downwards, and therefore the angles of the collar, which stand up for decoration, are not considered a corner of the garment. And although some hold that it is right to round one of the points (Magen Avraham 10:13; Mishna Berura 36), Israel have been accustomed to be lenient in this without hesitation.<sup>3</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":1,"en":"According to the Re'em (401), Rabbeinu Simḥa and the Ittur, a garment with five corners is exempt; but according to the Rif, the Rambam, the Rosh and the great majority of the Rishonim it is obliged, and one even blesses over a garment of five corners (Beit Yosef and Shulḥan Arukh 10:1). But ideally it is better to make a garment of four corners (Baḥ; Mishna Berura 10:3; Ben Ish Ḥai, Noaḥ 6)."},{"n":2,"en":"From the Rashba (1:434) it emerges that only a tallit that is entirely open is obliged in tzitzit. But according to the Maharik (148), all the Rishonim, including the Rashba, hold that even if the garment is sewn at the top like an undershirt or a shirt, if most of it is open it is considered to have four corners and is obliged in tzitzit. And so ruled the Shulḥan Arukh (10:12). And so the Aḥaronim agreed. On this basis they began to make undershirts most of which is open, in order to combine the tzitzit in a garment one wears anyway, and in order to encourage more people to fulfil the mitzva. Later they began to make a 'shirt-tzitzit' that also has sleeves, and is open for the most part, and has snap buttons that close it so that it can serve as a shirt. However, according to Responsa Binyan Av (5:4), one should take into account the view of the Rashba (and not as the Maharik understood him), and because of the doubt one should not bless over it, and consequently one should not make it. In his view, even one who does not take the Rashba's view into account should refrain from fitting buttons to this garment, because according to the Levush, the Magen Avraham (10:12) and the Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 18, it is forbidden to make hooks to close a garment most of which is open, even if they are not fastened tight, because of 'appearances', since it will look as though he put tzitzit on a garment that has no four corners. On the other hand, Rav Eliyahu wrote (Ma'amar Mordekhai 1:1) that as long as it is recognisable to onlookers that most of the garment is open, and it is closed by buttons or a zip, there is no concern of appearances. Thus far. And it seems that today even the Levush and those who agree with him would agree to this, for anyone who sees these garments understands that they were made especially for tzitzit, and consequently understands that most of them is open."},{"n":3,"en":"A further question was asked about the 'frock' coat, common among rabbis and dayanim, which has a large slit at the back, so that most of it is open, and so it has two corners in front and two behind. Some say that since its shape is that of a closed garment and not of a tallit half of which is open (like a tzitzit undershirt), it is exempt from tzitzit (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 10:16-19). And some say it is indeed obliged in tzitzit, but if one of the corners is rounded it will be exempt (see Mishna Berura 10:36). And such is the practice: to round one of the points at the back."}]},{"slug":"21-02-03","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-03/","title_he":"ג – גודל הבגד","title_en":"3. The Size of the Garment","body_en":"<p>The mitzva to put on tzitzit is on a garment and a covering, that is, a garment meant to cover the body, as it is said (Bamidbar 15:38): \"on the corners of their garments\", and it is said (Devarim 22:12): \"on the four corners of your covering with which you cover yourself.\"</p>\n<p>The Sages said (Menaḥot 40b) that for the cloth to be considered a garment that covers the body, it must have a measure \"with which a child covers his head and most of his body, and an adult goes out in it occasionally\". 'Child' means one of about nine, and the garment must cover his head and most of his body, the arms and legs not being counted as part of the body. Cloth of this measure is a garment which one as big as a thirteen-year-old can already use occasionally in his dress. Therefore it has the status of a 'garment', and consequently even a person with a large body who wishes to go about in it must put tzitzit on it. In practice, according to many, a garment whose length is one and a half amot (about 70 cm) and whose width is an ama (about 46 cm) is obliged in tzitzit with a blessing. And those who are meticulous take a garment whose length is two amot (about 92 cm) and whose width is an ama (about 46 cm). A tallit of this measure covers most of the body of a big person.<sup>4</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":4,"en":"Menaḥot (40b): \"A tallit with which a child covers his head and most of his body, and in which an adult goes out occasionally, is obliged in tzitzit. If a child does not cover his head and most of his body with it, even though an adult goes out in it occasionally, it is exempt.\" The Rambam (Tzitzit 3:1), the Roke'aḥ (361) and the Orḥot Ḥayim (Laws of Tzitzit 21) did not mention the condition that an adult goes out in it occasionally. And they explained that this is not an additional condition but the reason for the law: any garment with which a child covers his head and most of his body is obliged in tzitzit because an adult usually goes out in it occasionally (R. Yitzḥak Abuhav, brought in the Beit Yosef; Olat Tamid 1). And such is the view of the Shulḥan Arukh (16:1). In contrast, the Semak (31), the Rid, the Riaz, Rabbeinu Yeruḥam and the Tur brought both measures. And the Baḥ, the Peri Megadim and others explained that in their view there are indeed two conditions: (a) that a child covers his head and most of his body with it; (b) that an adult of thirteen is not ashamed to go out to the market in it. And if only one of the conditions is fulfilled, it is exempt. And so wrote the Rema. And many Aḥaronim wrote that it is obliged in tzitzit but one should not bless over it (Mishna Berura 16:4).<br>According to the Tur and the Beit Yosef (explaining the Rambam), a child is one of nine, and such is the view of most of the Aḥaronim. However, some consider a child to be one of six or seven (the Rambam and the Ra'avad as understood by the Ḥinukh 386, and the Semak as understood by the Baḥ). And some say one of five (Radbaz 4, 2,106).<br>In practice, some hold that its measure is three quarters of an ama by half an ama (Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh 9:1); some hold an ama by an ama (Peri Ha-Aretz 1:1; Beit David, Oraḥ Ḥayim 8; Neḥpeh Ba-Kesef 1; Ha-Elef Lekha Shlomo, Oraḥ Ḥayim 4); some hold an ama and a half by an ama (Artzot Ha-Ḥayim 16:4; Rav Pe'alim II, Oraḥ Ḥayim 6); and some are stringent, two amot by an ama without counting the neck hole (Siddur Ha-Rav). In practice, there were those who were very concerned and wrote that one should not bless over a 'tallit katan' because people are ashamed to go out to the market in it, but should exempt it with the blessing over the 'tallit gadol' (Eshel Avraham of Butshatsh; Yeḥaveh Da'at 5:2). But in practice the halakha follows the view of the great majority of the poskim, who oblige a garment of an ama and a half by an ama (Rav Eliyahu, Ma'amar Mordekhai for weekdays 7:78), for such a garment covers the head and most of the body of a nine-year-old, and also covers most of the body of a thirteen-year-old, both on the side of his back and on the side of his stomach, and he is not ashamed to go out in it. And if there are those who are ashamed of it, this is not because of its size but because people are not used to going about in a 'tallit katan' over their clothes (Bi'ur Halakha 16:1, s.v. 'La-shuk'). And those who are meticulous take a 'tallit katan' of the measure of two amot by an ama, and so that the neck hole too is counted, they take care that the measure of cloth at the shoulders be greater than the neck opening; and those who are meticulous also make a triangle-shaped opening, so that in practice, although the cloth there folds over the body, since it is part of the tallit it joins the measure. On the measures of the ama, see Peninei Halakha, Sukkot 2, note 1. And one need not be concerned in this for the measure of the Noda Bi-Yehuda and the Ḥazon Ish, for the Aḥaronim who wrote an ama and a half spoke of the measure to which they were accustomed."}]},{"slug":"21-02-04","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-04/","title_he":"ד – ייעודו של הבגד","title_en":"4. The Purpose of the Garment","body_en":"<p>A scarf, a turban or a keffiyeh are exempt from tzitzit, since they are meant for covering the head and the neck, whereas the garment obliged in tzitzit is a garment meant for covering the body. And even if from time to time they wrap most of the body in them, since they are meant for covering the head or the neck, they are exempt from tzitzit (Shulḥan Arukh 10:11-12).<sup>5</sup></p>\n<p>A towel that has four corners, although at times one coming out of the shower wraps himself in it to cover himself or to warm himself in it, is not obliged in tzitzit, since it is not meant for covering the body and therefore is not considered a garment. Similarly, a vest against the enemy (body armour), an apron against radiation, an apron for protection from dirt during work, or an apron for a haircut are exempt from tzitzit, since they are meant for protection from specific things, and therefore they are not considered a garment meant for protection from cold or heat or for dignity (see Bi'ur Halakha 10:11, s.v. 'Sudar'). Similarly, those who wrap themselves in the national flag are exempt from tzitzit, since the flag is not a garment, and they wrap themselves in it for the sake of identification and not as clothing and covering like a garment.</p>\n<p>A garment is something meant to cover a person as he walks in his home and outside his home. Therefore blankets, even though one sleeps in them by day, are exempt from tzitzit, since they are not a garment (the Mordekhai; Shulḥan Arukh 18:2). Indeed there were those who were concerned lest blankets in which one sleeps by day too are obliged in tzitzit, and therefore they took care to round one of their corners, especially if they are of wool (Magen Avraham 18:3; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 5; Kaf Ha-Ḥayim 17). But the custom is not to be stringent in this, for the main halakha is that a blanket is not a garment and is exempt from tzitzit (Mor U-Ketzi'a; Eshel Avraham of Butshatsh; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 18:8; Berit Kehuna, ma'arekhet tzadi 14). And such is the law of a blanket in which one sometimes wraps oneself on the sofa and the like (a 'throw'), for it is meant to serve as a blanket and not as a garment.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":5,"en":"According to the Kaftor Va-Feraḥ (60), a turban or a scarf with which people are accustomed to cover the body too, and which has the measure of a tallit, although their main purpose is to cover the head or the neck, is obliged in tzitzit. And so wrote Artzot Ha-Ḥayim (10:42), and he wrote to round one of the corners and thereby exempt them. But in the view of many one should not put tzitzit on them, because a garment meant for the head or the neck is a 'covering of the head and neck' and not a covering in general, which is a covering of the body, and consequently it is exempt from tzitzit (Beit Yosef 10:10-11; Levush 10). And such is the widespread custom. And those who are meticulous make one rounded corner on a scarf or a turban with which people are accustomed to cover the body too (Bi'ur Halakha 10:10, s.v. 'Keivan she-ikara'; and see Maharsham 3:272). And one who takes an especially wide scarf and is accustomed to cover a little of his body with it too should properly be stringent in this."}]},{"slug":"21-02-05","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-05/","title_he":"ה – חומר הטלית","title_en":"5. The Material of the Tallit","body_en":"<p>The Amoraim disagreed on the question whether all kinds of cloth are obliged in tzitzit from the Torah (Menaḥot 39b). Some say that every square garment of any kind of cloth is obliged in tzitzit from the Torah, as it is said (Bamidbar 15:38): \"that they shall make for themselves tzitzit on the corners of their garments\" (Rema). And some say that since garments of wool and linen were the common cloths in the past, the Torah's intention is that these cloths are obliged in tzitzit. And the Sages instituted that garments of other cloths too, such as cotton or silk, are obliged in tzitzit (Shulḥan Arukh 9:1). In practice, in order to fulfil the mitzva of tzitzit from the Torah according to all views, the custom is to be meticulous that the 'tallit gadol' be of wool, and some are meticulous that the 'tallit katan' worn under the shirt too be of wool. However, one who is hot in a woollen 'tallit katan' may ideally wear a 'tallit katan' of other cloths, for even a mitzva about which there is a dispute whether its obligation is from the Torah or rabbinic is of very great virtue.<sup>6</sup></p>\n<p>The custom is not to make a tallit or threads of tzitzit from linen, out of concern that one may come to the prohibition of sha'atnez (see below halakha 13). However, when necessary, when there is no possibility of making a tallit and threads of wool or cotton and the like, it is right to make them of linen, while taking care to make both the tallit and the threads of linen (Shulḥan Arukh and Rema 9:2; 6).<sup>7</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":6,"en":"In Menaḥot (39b), according to Rav Naḥman (following the Tanna of the school of Rabbi Yishmael), only a garment of wool or linen is obliged in tzitzit from the Torah, and the other kinds by rabbinic law; and so wrote the Rif, the Rambam (Tzitzit 3:1-2), the Ra'avan and the Ḥinukh (386), because in the Gemara Shabbat (26b-27a) they discussed the words of the Tanna of the school of Rabbi Yishmael. And so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (9:1). And according to Rav Yehuda and Rava, every garment is obliged in tzitzit from the Torah, and so wrote Tosafot (Menaḥot 39b, s.v. 'Ve-Rav Naḥman') in the name of Rashi and Rabbeinu Tam; the Rid, the Ittur, the Semag, the Rosh, the Ritva and others, because Rava is the later authority, and in addition this is the view of most of the Amoraim. And so wrote the Rema (9:1).<br>In order to fulfil the mitzva from the Torah according to all views, it is fitting that the tallit be of wool (Eliya Rabba, Peri Megadim, Mishna Berura 9:5). And the Pele Yo'etz wrote ('Tzitzit') that one who wears a tallit that is not of wool \"is like one who could earn a thousand gold coins and is content with five hundred\". However, one who is hot in a woollen 'tallit katan' may ideally wear a 'tallit katan' of other cloths, for even a mitzva about which there is a dispute whether its force is from the Torah or rabbinic is of very great virtue. And there were great ones of Israel who acted so, such as the Gra (Ma'aseh Rav 17). Some wrote that those of Sephardi origin must be more careful about wool, since according to the Shulḥan Arukh only a garment of wool is obliged from the Torah (Or Le-Tziyon II 2:3). But in truth there is no difference in this between the customs, for in practice many of the Ashkenazi poskim encouraged wearing a 'tallit katan' of wool, and on the other hand there were Sephardi poskim who did not encourage wearing a 'tallit katan' at all. In practice too we have not found a difference between those of the various communities, and one should not create disputes for nothing. In addition, according to all views there is no obligation to wear a 'tallit katan' (above 1:3), all the more so there is no obligation to be meticulous that it be of wool, for even one who wears a tallit of cotton is beautifying the mitzva, since the whole aim of wearing it is for the sake of the mitzva."},{"n":7,"en":"From the Torah a linen garment is obliged in tzitzit, and it is a mitzva to put on it threads of tekhelet, which were made of wool, for the mitzva of tzitzit overrides the prohibition of sha'atnez (Menaḥot 40a; Shabbat 25b). But the Sages decreed not to put tekhelet on a linen garment, lest people put tzitzit on a 'night garment', which is exempt from tzitzit, and transgress the prohibition of sha'atnez. According to the Rif, Rashi, the Rambam (Tzitzit 3:7) and others, the prohibition is to put threads of tekhelet on a linen garment, but one must put white threads of linen on it. And according to the Geonim, Rabbeinu Tam (Shabbat 25b, s.v. 'Sadin'), the Ma'or, the Nimukei Yosef and others, because of the concern of sha'atnez the Sages entirely exempted a linen garment from the mitzva of tzitzit, and one who blesses over it makes a blessing in vain. The Rosh wrote (Responsa 2:8) that in Spain they used to put tzitzit on a linen garment, and he justified them as following the Rif and those who agree with him, and perhaps even according to Rabbeinu Tam, when there is no tekhelet there is no need to be concerned for the decree. But ideally one takes into account the view of the Geonim and Rabbeinu Tam, and does not go about in a square linen garment (Shulḥan Arukh and Rema 9:6; Mishna Berura 17). However, one who has no other garment should put linen tzitzit on a linen tallit, and even bless over it, since this is the main halakha (Radbaz 2:626; Mishna Berura 19).<br>Linen threads, like wool threads, exempt all the kinds (Menaḥot 39b; below, beginning of halakha 6). And so wrote the Rambam, the Terumat Ha-Deshen and the Shulḥan Arukh (9:2). However, ideally one does not put on linen threads, because one takes into account the view of the Maharam (Responsa 444), who holds that linen threads do not exempt other kinds unless there is tekhelet with them. And further, lest people put them on a garment of wool (Rema 9:2; Mishna Berura 8)."}]},{"slug":"21-02-06","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-06/","title_he":"ו – חומר חוטי הציצית","title_en":"6. The Material of the Tzitzit Threads","body_en":"<p>Tzitzit threads of wool or linen exempt all kinds of cloth, but threads of other kinds exempt only their own kind. For example, cotton threads exempt a garment of cotton and not a garment of wool or silk, but wool threads exempt a garment of cotton and of silk too (Shulḥan Arukh 9:2-3). As we learned in the previous halakha, the custom is not to use threads of linen.</p>\n<p>Some hold that the colour of the threads should be like the colour of the garment, that is, if the garment is white the threads should be white, and if the garment is red they should be red, for this is the beauty of the mitzva, and in this way too the tzitzit are 'of the kind of the corner'. According to their view, the white threads are called white because in fact most garments were white; but when the garment is dyed, the 'white' threads should be the colour of the garment (Rashi, Rambam, Shulḥan Arukh 9:5). On the other hand, some hold that even when the garment is dyed one may make it threads of white colour (the Semag, the Rashba and the Terumat Ha-Deshen), and some say that one must make it white threads (the Ba'al Ha-Ittur and the Rema). In order to avoid the doubt, it is preferable to make the garment white, and so according to all views one makes it white threads. And there is even a virtue in this, because white hints at the attribute of kindness. Even when the garment has black or blue stripes, as is accepted, it is considered according to its majority as a white garment, especially since the corner itself is white.<sup>8</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":8,"en":"Menaḥot (41b): \"Our Rabbis taught: a tallit that is entirely tekhelet – all kinds of colours exempt it except indigo\", and on the other hand it is brought there: \"A tallit – only its own kind exempts it.\" Many of the Rishonim explained that the threads should be the colour of the garment, and only if the colour of the garment is tekhelet should the 'white' threads be made white or another colour, so that there will be a difference between the tekhelet and the white. And so wrote Rashi, the Rambam and the Ra'avad (Tzitzit 2:8), the Meiri (Yevamot 4b), the Aguda (Menaḥot 38b), the Orḥot Ḥayim (I, Tzitzit 38), the Mordekhai (948). According to Tosafot and the Mordekhai, the reason is 'This is my God and I will glorify Him'. And according to Rashi, the Ra'avad and the Meiri, it is because of the derivation: 'the corner – the kind of the corner'. And there is one who says that according to Rashi and those who agree with him, if he made them not in the colour of the corner he has not fulfilled his obligation (Ben Yedid, Tzitzit 2:8). And some hold that even according to them he has fulfilled his obligation after the fact (Taharat Ha-Kodesh, Menaḥot 41b; Mayim Ḥayim of Messas, Oraḥ Ḥayim 1:5).<br>On the other hand, according to the Semag (positive commandments 26) and the Maharam (Hagahot Maimoniyot 3:5), one may make the threads of any colour, and all that was learned from 'the kind of the corner' is that the threads must be of the material of the garment. Similarly the Rashba (3:280) and the Terumat Ha-Deshen (46) wrote that the simple custom is to make white threads even for a coloured garment. And so it emerges from the words of the Rid (Menaḥot 41b). And the Ba'al Ha-Ittur (2:2) wrote that even on a coloured garment one must make white threads, and only after the fact, if he has no white, should he make threads in the colour of the garment. And they added and explained that there is a virtue in the tallit and the tzitzit being white, corresponding to the attribute of kindness (Yam Shel Shlomo, Yevamot 1:3, in the name of the Mahari Bruna; Sha'ar Ha-Kavanot, Derushei Ha-Tzitzit 2; Maggid Meisharim, Shelaḥ).<br>The Shulḥan Arukh (9:5) wrote that some say one must make the tzitzit of the colour of the garment, so that if it is coloured the threads will be coloured, \"and the meticulous act so\". And such is the view of the Baḥ and the Olat Tamid, but they wrote that in this case he should conceal the tzitzit so that he does not appear to be acting against the custom. And the Rema remarked that according to the Ashkenazi custom one makes white threads even on a coloured garment, \"and one should not change\". And those of Sephardi origin too acted so (Mayim Ḥayim of Messas, Oraḥ Ḥayim 1:5; Kaf Ha-Ḥayim 15:36).<br>In order to satisfy all views, they ruled to make a white garment and white threads (Taz 9:8; Shiyurei Berakha 5; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 9; Artzot Ha-Ḥayim 5; Mishna Berura 15; Kaf Ha-Ḥayim 5). Some are meticulous to make the tallit entirely white (the Maharshal, Ḥesed La-Alafim, the Ḥida). But in the view of most of the poskim it is enough that the main part of the garment be white, as is the custom of the meticulous to decorate the tallit with black or blue stripes near its edges as a remembrance of the tekhelet (Leket Yosher I, 12; Peri Megadim, Eshel Avraham 9:6; Mishna Berura 9:16; Ben Ish Ḥai, Noaḥ 11). The flag of the State of Israel was designed on the basis of this shape of tallit.<br>Soldiers who for reasons of camouflage must wear a green tallit: if there is no operational value in the threads being green, it is preferable that they make white threads according to the custom, even though the tallit is green."}]},{"slug":"21-02-07","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-07/","title_he":"ז – בגד מעור וחוטים סינטטיים","title_en":"7. A Garment of Leather, and Synthetic Threads","body_en":"<p>A leather garment is exempt from tzitzit, because it is not made by weaving threads in the way of garments, but is made of a single piece (Shulḥan Arukh 10:4; Levush; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav). And likewise a sheet of nylon or plastic from which a kind of garment is made, meant to protect workers in their work, is exempt from tzitzit, since it is not made of threads.</p>\n<p>Some say that a garment made of synthetic threads too is exempt from tzitzit (Igrot Moshe, Oraḥ Ḥayim 2:1). But in the view of most of the Aḥaronim it is obliged in tzitzit, for there is no difference between a garment made of natural threads and a garment made of synthetic threads; rather, anything made of threads is obliged in tzitzit (Har Tzvi, Oraḥ Ḥayim 1:9). But because of the doubt there were those who ruled not to bless over tzitzit on such a garment (Tzitz Eliezer 12:3). However, their words were said of the synthetic threads of the past, whose quality was poor, which did not keep one warm in winter and in which one sweated in summer, and only because of their low price were they used to make cheap garments. But over the years the quality of synthetic threads improved wonderfully, and today high-quality garments are made of them, which at times are even considered better than garments of natural threads. Therefore one should bless over a tallit of synthetic threads (such as Dri-FIT).<sup>9</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":9,"en":"Some say that a garment made of synthetic threads is exempt from tzitzit like leather, since it could have been made as a single casting (Maharai Steif 28; Igrot Moshe, Oraḥ Ḥayim 2:1). And some hold that it is obliged, but out of concern for the view of those who exempt it they ruled not to bless (Tzitz Eliezer 12:3; Mayim Ḥayim of R. Ḥayim David Ha-Levi 2:2). And many hold that if the synthetic garment is made of threads that are woven or knitted, its law is like a garment over which one must bless (Har Tzvi, Oraḥ Ḥayim 1:9; Rav Goren in Meshiv Milḥama 2:126; Halikhot Shlomo, Prayer 3:16; Ma'amar Mordekhai for weekdays 7:68; Melumdei Milḥama 112, who wrote that so held R. Yosef Eliyahu Henkin). Therefore one must bless over a four-cornered Dri-FIT undershirt made of knitted threads. However, a mesh tallit made of synthetic material by casting: many held that it is exempt from tzitzit, for its law is like that of leather, since it was not woven from threads (Har Tzvi there, Mayim Ḥayim there, Meshiv Milḥama there, and so it seems from Rav Eliyahu and Rav Auerbach and many others who copied the Har Tzvi). But it seems more correct that it too is obliged in tzitzit with a blessing, since it is cast like a garment made of threads, through which air passes, unlike leather, through which air does not pass. And so wrote Az Nidberu (7:52) and Ḥemda Genuza (1:18) of Rav David Shloush. And there is room to say that as the manufacture of garments develops, if this way becomes one of the main ways of making garments, even those who exempt it will agree that one must bless over such a garment."}]},{"slug":"21-02-08","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-08/","title_he":"ח – הכנת חוטי הציצית לשם מצווה","title_en":"8. Preparing the Tzitzit Threads for the Sake of the Mitzva","body_en":"<p>The preparation of the threads for tzitzit must be done for the sake of the mitzva of tzitzit, as it is said (Devarim 22:12): \"You shall make for yourself twisted threads\", which the Sages expounded: \"'for yourself' – for the sake of your obligation\" (Sukka 9a). The work of preparing the tzitzit threads consists of three stages: (a) combing (nipputz), which is carding the tangled wool and softening it; (b) spinning, in which the fibres of the wool are twisted and turned around themselves until they become a thread; (c) plying, in which one takes at least two threads and twists them together in order to strengthen the thread and balance it. Both before the spinning and before the plying one must say \"for the sake of the mitzva of tzitzit\", and ideally it is good to say it before the combing too. This declaration is effective for the whole of that stage, even if it lasts several days.</p>\n<p>If he spun the threads not for their own sake, the threads are invalid, since the spinning is the essence of making the threads. If he plied the threads not for their own sake, in a pressing situation the tzitzit are valid and one even blesses over them, since even without plying the thread exists.<sup>10</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":10,"en":"The essence of making the threads is by spinning. According to Rav there is no need to spin the threads for their own sake, and according to Shmuel one must spin them for their own sake from the Torah (Menaḥot 42b), and so it was ruled in halakha, that threads spun not for their own sake are invalid for tzitzit (Shulḥan Arukh, Oraḥ Ḥayim 11:1).<br>According to the Ra'avan, the Ra'avya and the Rosh, following the Sifrei (Shelaḥ 115), the spun tzitzit threads also require plying. And the Kol Bo explained that this is \"for the sake of beauty\". And according to the Rambam they do not require plying. In halakha the Shulḥan Arukh (11:2) ruled that they require plying. The Aḥaronim wrote, following the Nimukei Yosef, that one must ply them with intention for the sake of tzitzit. Some say that after the fact, if the threads were not plied, they are valid for tzitzit (Eliya Rabba 11:2; Mor U-Ketzi'a; Maḥazik Berakha 11:2; Sha'arei Teshuva 2). On the other hand, some invalidate them if they were not plied for their own sake (Magen Avraham 11:3; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 3; Magen Giborim 11; Ḥayei Adam 11:14). And according to the middle view, threads that are not plied are invalid for tzitzit, but if they were plied not for their own sake they are valid (Derekh Ha-Ḥayim; Artzot Ha-Ḥayim 11; and see Mishna Berura 11:15).<br>A thread whose plying came apart: the part that is not plied is considered invalid; but usually the thread is plied of eight strands, and therefore if it came apart into two or four, each part still remains plied and valid (Shulḥan Arukh 11:3; Mishna Berura 17). However, so as not to transgress 'do not add', he should say that he does not want the additional strands, or cut them (Bi'ur Halakha there, s.v. 'Ve-na'asu'). According to the Shulḥan Arukh (11:14) and the Rema (11:3), it is good to tie each thread at its end, in order to prevent its plying from coming apart. And the Levush (11:14) wrote that the custom is not to do so, perhaps because the plying of the threads was good and there was no need to tie them. And the Magen Avraham 23 wrote that it looks like adding to the knots. Therefore, when there is no concern that their plying will come apart, it is right not to tie them (Mishna Berura 18). And if he glues the ends of the threads, it will not look like adding to the knots.<br>In order to make threads one must first comb (card) the wool. In the name of the Maharam of Rothenburg they said that it is right to comb for its own sake (the Mordekhai and the Maharil), and the Agur wrote (Laws of Tzitzit 23) that \"so the conscientious of Ashkenaz act\". And some ruled so ideally (Baḥ 11:3; Derisha 1, in the name of the Maharal of Prague; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 4; Mishna Berura 3). But the Beit Yosef wrote: \"We have not seen anyone who was concerned about this\", and the Rema wrote (11:1): \"And some are stringent even to comb them for their own sake, and the custom is to be lenient regarding combing.\" And so wrote many Aḥaronim (Levush, Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 3, and others)."}]},{"slug":"21-02-09","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-09/","title_he":"ט – נשים, נוכרים ומכונה בהכנת החוטים וקשירתם","title_en":"9. Women, Gentiles and Machines in Preparing and Tying the Threads","body_en":"<p>All the tasks that require intention in preparing the threads must be done by a Jew, for on Jews, both men and women, one relies that they will have the intention for the sake of the mitzva (Shulḥan Arukh 11:2).</p>\n<p>In recent generations the question arose whether one may prepare the tzitzit threads by a machine, when it is operated for the sake of the mitzva. In the view of most of the Aḥaronim, machine threads are valid for tzitzit, because the work need not be done by a person but only with intention, and since a Jew operated the machine with intention for the sake of the mitzva of tzitzit, the whole action that follows from it is considered as done with intention. And some forbid, because in their view the whole work of making the threads must be done by a person and with intention, and what the machine does is not an act of a person and is not done with intention.</p>\n<p>In practice, it is right to be meticulous and fulfil the mitzva with tzitzit made by hand, in which the intention is complete and of the finest kind, and with which one fulfils the obligation according to all views; but machine tzitzit too are valid, and one may even bless over them, provided that the machine was operated for the sake of the mitzva of tzitzit.<sup>11</sup></p>\n<p>Putting the tzitzit threads into the garment and tying them into the gedil must be done by a Jew and not by a gentile, as it is said (Bamidbar 15:38): \"Speak to the children of Israel… that they shall make for themselves tzitzit\", which the Sages expounded (Menaḥot 42a): \"The children of Israel shall make them, and not gentiles shall make them\" (Shulḥan Arukh 14:1).</p>\n<p>In the view of most of the poskim, just as women are fit to prepare the tzitzit threads, so they are fit to put them on and tie them (Shulḥan Arukh 14:1). And some are stringent, holding that only one who is obliged in tzitzit is fit for this. Ideally one is stringent to have the tzitzit threads put on and tied by males who are bar mitzva (Rema).<sup>12</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":11,"en":"A gentile who prepared threads while a Jew stood over him and told him to make them for the sake of the mitzva: according to the Rambam the threads are invalid, for one does not rely on the gentile to have this intention, and according to the Rosh the threads are valid, for one relies on him to act according to the Jew's words (Shulḥan Arukh 11:2). In the Responsa of Rabbi Akiva Eiger (first edition 4) it is explained that if a convert testified that he made them for their own sake while still a gentile, he is believed at least regarding himself. Thus far. This implies that if one is sure that the gentile is trustworthy, one relies on his words that he spun them for their own sake. (There is a view in the Gemara that a gentile is fit to put on and tie the tzitzit threads, and so ruled the Ittur; but according to the other Rishonim he is not fit, and so it was ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh 14:1.)<br>About two hundred and fifty years ago the first machines for spinning threads began to be invented. The Aḥaronim disagreed whether one may make threads for tzitzit by machine. The dispute concerns two matters: (a) whether, by a Jew operating the machine for the sake of the mitzva, the spinning of the threads is considered 'for its own sake'; (b) whether 'human force' is needed in spinning the threads, and if so, whether the action of the machine is considered 'human force'. Among those who permit: Ḥesed Le-Avraham (second edition, Oraḥ Ḥayim 3), Rav Yosef Shaul Nathansohn (Kuntres Gedolei Gedolim), Aḥi'ezer (3:69), Har Tzvi (Oraḥ Ḥayim 1:10), Ḥazon Ish (Oraḥ Ḥayim 6:10), Tzitz Eliezer (6:15). And our master Rav Kook ztz\"l in Oraḥ Mishpat, Oraḥ Ḥayim 122, justified those who are lenient. Among those who forbid: Imrei Esh (Oraḥ Ḥayim 1), Divrei Ḥayim (II 1-2), Maḥazeh Avraham 3, Minḥat Yitzḥak (2:96-99). (See Peninei Halakha, Pesaḥ 12:4, on the dispute about machine matzot, which is similar to this dispute.)"},{"n":12,"en":"Women are fit to put on and tie the tzitzit threads on the garment (Tosafot Menaḥot 42a, s.v. 'Minayin'; Semag, positive commandments 26; the Teruma, the Mordekhai, and the Rosh, Laws of Tzitzit 13). And so it was ruled in the Shulḥan Arukh (14:1). However, some are stringent and hold that women cannot tie tzitzit, because it is said \"Speak to the children (benei) of Israel\" (the Maharam). And some are stringent because only one who is obliged in tzitzit may tie them, and women, who are exempt from tzitzit, may not tie them on the garment (Rabbeinu Tam). The Rema wrote that ideally it is good to be stringent. According to Artzot Ha-Ḥayim one takes into account only the view of the Maharam, and therefore children who know how to have intention may tie tzitzit. But some take into account the view of Rabbeinu Tam, and then children too are unfit (Mishna Berura 14:4).<br>Some are stringent ideally not to put on tzitzit at night (Naḥalat Yosef of Meisels, Oraḥ Ḥayim 21:11; Ashrei Ha-Ish 1:2:22), but in the view of the great majority of the poskim it is permitted ideally to put on tzitzit at night (Mishbetzot Zahav 18:1; Ḥokhmat Shlomo 14:1; Lev Ḥayim 3:29; Ben Ish Ḥai, Lekh Lekha 18; Halikhot Shlomo 3:23; Yabi'a Omer 8:3; Revavot Efrayim 3:27, and many others).<br>The Sages forbade selling a tasseled tallit to a gentile, lest with the help of the tzitzit he disguise himself as a Jew, accompany him on his way and in the end kill him (Menaḥot 43a; Rambam and Shulḥan Arukh 20:2). In a place where the gentiles behave morally and there is no concern that they will murder, there is no prohibition (Peninei Halakha, Emuna and Its Mitzvot 3:11)."}]},{"slug":"21-02-10","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-10/","title_he":"י – הטלת הציציות בבגד","title_en":"10. Putting the Tzitzit on the Garment","body_en":"<p>On each of the four corners of the tallit one must put tzitzit. The Sages learned that the mitzva of the Torah is that on each corner four threads be tied (Menaḥot 42a; Shulḥan Arukh 11:12).</p>\n<p>The threads are attached to the tallit by inserting the four threads into the hole in the corner of the garment, and when they come out of the other side of the hole they become doubled, so that in practice there are eight threads in the tzitzit.</p>\n<p>Some say that putting on the tzitzit and tying them does not require intention, but it is enough that a Jew does it (Rambam), and some say that it requires intention for the sake of the mitzva of tzitzit, and so is the practice (Shulḥan Arukh 14:2). In practice, every Jew who puts tzitzit on the four corners of a garment does so for the sake of the mitzva, but the enhancement is to say explicitly before beginning to put the tzitzit on the first corner: \"I am tying the tzitzit for the sake of the mitzva\" (see Bi'ur Halakha, s.v. 'Be-lo kavana').</p>\n<p>If there was a garment with three corners and a fourth round corner, and he put tzitzit on its three corners and afterwards added a fourth corner to it by sewing or by cutting and put tzitzit on it, it is invalid. Because it is said (Devarim 22:12): \"You shall make for yourself twisted threads on the four corners of your covering\", which the Sages expounded: \"'You shall make' – and not from what is already made.\" That is, the making of the tzitzit for its mitzva must be fulfilled by tying the threads onto the garment, and not as the result of another action on the garment or the threads. Therefore he must untie the tzitzit on its three corners, and after the garment is square and becomes obliged in tzitzit, he should tie tzitzit on its four corners (Menaḥot 40b).</p>\n<p>The hole through which the threads are passed should be at a distance of about 5 cm from the edge of the garment: not more than three fingers (5.7 cm) and not less than 'the knuckle of the thumb' (3.6 cm), as it is said (Bamidbar 15:38): \"that they shall make for themselves tzitzit on the corners of their garments\". If it is too close to the edge of the garment it will be below the corner, and if it is too far it will be in the garment itself and not on the corner (Menaḥot 42a; Shulḥan Arukh 11:9-10).<sup>13</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":13,"en":"See Middot Ve-Shiurei Torah of Rav Benish (7:22). Many are accustomed, for decoration, to continue the warp threads at the edges of the garment without weft, and tie them at their end. Some are concerned that perhaps they are considered part of the garment for measuring the 'three fingers', and therefore are accustomed not to make them at the corners of the garment (Mishna Berura 11:57). And those of Yemenite origin are not concerned about this and braid the warp threads that continue from the garment even at the corners of the garment (Rav Kafiḥ, Tzitzit 1:13). The widespread custom is to make one hole into which the tzitzit are inserted, and they try that the tzitzit be 'dripping over the edge', that is, hanging over the side of the garment and from there extending downwards, to fulfil 'on the corners of their garments' (Shulḥan Arukh 11:15). In order to keep the tzitzit in this position, some are accustomed to tighten the knot a little on the garment, and although by this he slightly contracts the garment, this does not reduce it below the measure of the knuckle of the thumb, since if it is stretched out it has the measure (Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 11:19). For this reason some are accustomed to make two holes in a 'tallit katan' and pass the tzitzit threads through both and then tie them, and so they will necessarily drip over the corner of the garment (see Mishna Berura 11:39).<br>If the hole on which the tzitzit hangs tore, and the measure of 'the knuckle of the thumb' does not remain up to the edge of the garment: since the tzitzit were tied when the hole was more than 'the knuckle of the thumb' from the edge of the garment, the tzitzit are valid. But if the hole tore entirely to its edge, there is no possibility of making the tallit valid by sewing it up with the gedil, because of 'You shall make, and not from what is already made'; rather, one must sew up the edge of the garment and only afterwards tie the tzitzit to it anew. Therefore, when the hole begins to tear, it is good to sew it and strengthen it. And one should not sew it with a thread similar to the tzitzit threads (Shulḥan Arukh 15:5; Mishna Berura 20)."}]},{"slug":"21-02-11","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-11/","title_he":"יא – הגדיל והפתילים","title_en":"11. The Gedil and the Threads","body_en":"<p>It is a mitzva to tie the threads of the tzitzit in such a way that at first the threads of the tzitzit are wound and tied as a gedil, as it is said (Devarim 22:12): \"You shall make for yourself twisted threads (gedilim) on the four corners of your covering.\" And out of the gedil the threads of the tzitzit extend like branches, in such a way that the threads are separate from one another, as it is said (Bamidbar 15:38): \"and they shall put on the tzitzit of the corner a thread\" (Menaḥot 39b).</p>\n<p>From the Torah there is no measure for the length of the tzitzit threads, and whenever there is a gedil and a branch (separate threads) it is valid. Therefore, in order to fulfil the mitzva from the Torah, one must insert four threads into the hole in the corner of the tallit up to their middle, so that four threads come out of each side of the hole; tie the four with the four in a double knot; take one of the threads and wind it around the other threads at least three windings, which together are considered a 'ḥulya'; and tie the threads together again, so that the windings do not come apart. From there on the tzitzit threads are separate. However, in order to fulfil the mitzva properly, the Sages ruled to make the gedil with more windings and more knots, and the Sages further said (Menaḥot 39a) that ideally one should make a third of the tzitzit gedil and two thirds branch, that is, threads, for this is the beauty of the tzitzit. And the custom is that the length of the gedil and the branch together be at least about 24 cm. And many are accustomed to be meticulous in lengthening the tzitzit, and since the number of windings in the gedil is precise, it often turns out that the threads are much more than two thirds.<sup>14</sup></p>\n<p>The widespread custom among those of Ashkenazi and Sephardi origin is that the gedil consists of five knots, each knot double, so that it will hold. Between the five knots one winds four ḥulyot, that is, four series of windings. After the first knot, in the first ḥulya, one winds a series of seven windings; after the second knot one winds eight windings; after the third, eleven windings; and after the fourth, thirteen windings; and after them one ties the fifth knot. The total of the windings is 39, which is the numerical value of the Name 'Havaya' (26) and 'eḥad' (13) (the Ra'avad, Rav Natronai Gaon, Shulḥan Arukh 11:14; Mishna Berura 70; Ben Ish Ḥai, Lekh Lekha 1). According to the custom of those of Yemenite origin, following the Rambam (Tzitzit 1:7-8; Responsa 138), the gedil consists of seven or thirteen ḥulyot, in each of which there are three windings, with a space between one ḥulya and the next, and the tying is done by looping the winding thread under the windings in each ḥulya.<sup>15</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":14,"en":"From the Torah there is no measure for the gedil and the threads; rather, whenever there is a gedil and a branch it is valid (Kesef Mishneh, Tzitzit 1:18; Levush 11:4; Eliya Zuta 8; Mishna Berura 20; Bi'ur Halakha 11:4, s.v. 'Ein'. And see further at the beginning of the next note). It is explained in the Gemara (Menaḥot 39a) that making the upper knot is from the Torah and is indispensable. Some say it is the knot next to the corner (Rabbeinu Gershom, Tosafot, the Ra'avad, the Ran), and apparently the looping after it held by itself, like the custom of those of Yemenite origin. And some say it is the knot after the first gedil, by which the gedil is held (Rav Amram Gaon, Rashi on Menaḥot, the Rambam, the Semag, the Mordekhai, the Nimukei Yosef). And one takes both views into account (Gra 11:44).<br>The Sages ruled in Menaḥot (39a): \"The beauty of tekhelet: a third gedil and two thirds branch.\" And in Menaḥot (41b) they said regarding the length of the tzitzit, \"a third of four\". According to the Rambam and the Rosh, the measure of the gedil and the branch is 4 fingers. According to Rashi, the measure of the branch is four fingers, and consequently the gedil two fingers, six fingers in all. And according to Rabbeinu Tam and the Semag, the gedil is four fingers and consequently the branch is eight fingers, which together are 12 fingers, which are three tefaḥim, about 24 cm, and precisely 22.8 cm (see Peninei Halakha, Sukkot 2, note 1). The Shulḥan Arukh (11:4) mentioned the view of the Rambam and the Rosh anonymously and that of Rabbeinu Tam and the Semag as 'some say', and concluded that the practice follows them. After the fact many hold that if he made a gedil and branch of 4 fingers' length it is valid and one may bless over it, following the view of the Rambam and the Rosh; and further, it is possible that even according to Rabbeinu Tam the 12 fingers are not an obligation but for the beauty of the mitzva (Lev Ḥayim 79; Rabbi Akiva Eiger 12:3; Ben Ish Ḥai, Noaḥ 12). And some say he should not bless (Eliya Rabba 6, explaining the Levush; Peri Megadim, Mishbetzot Zahav 5; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 12:1; see Bi'ur Halakha, s.v. 'Ve-khen nohagin'). (And some are meticulous according to the view of the Noda Bi-Yehuda and the Ḥazon Ish, a tefaḥ of 9.6 cm, and a measure of tzitzit of 28.8 cm.)<br>As we learned (Menaḥot 39a): \"The beauty of tekhelet: a third gedil and two thirds branch\", that is, separate threads. According to the Rema (11:14), it is right to keep the ratio between them, so that a third is gedil. But according to the Shulḥan Arukh 11:4, one may lengthen them more. And some wrote that it is even preferable to lengthen them (Erekh Leḥem; Magen Avraham 6; Mishna Berura 21). And so wrote the Maharshal following the Semag, that ideally one may lengthen the threads so that they are much more than two thirds; the Mishna Berura (11:69) brought this and added that in any case it does not invalidate. And so the custom is to lengthen the threads far beyond two thirds. The Rosh wrote (Tzitzit 15) that for the beauty of the tzitzit they used to make the four ḥulyot equal, and in the ḥulyot that have few windings they space the windings further apart. And so wrote the Mishna Berura (11:68). In practice the widespread custom is not to be particular about this (Eliya Rabba 20; Kaf Ha-Ḥayim 45)."},{"n":15,"en":"In Menaḥot (39a), Rav said: \"Tekhelet that was wound over most of it is valid, and even if only one ḥulya was wound with it, it is valid.\" That is, the customs of the knots and windings do not invalidate the tzitzit, and whenever one made a gedil and a branch it is valid. Further on in the Gemara a baraita is brought that explains how one should ideally make the gedil. \"What is the measure of a ḥulya? It was taught: Rabbi says, enough that he winds, repeats and does it a third time… One who reduces should not reduce below seven, corresponding to the seven heavens, and one who adds should not add beyond thirteen, corresponding to the seven heavens and the six spaces between them.\" They said in the Midrash (Bamidbar Rabba 18:21) that around the ḥulyot one ties five knots in the gedil, and together with eight threads and six hundred as the numerical value of 'tzitzit', it comes to 613. Therefore the custom is to make four ḥulyot held by five double knots (so that they will hold).<br>Some say that when there is no tekhelet there is no particular concern about the number of windings and ḥulyot (Geonim; Tur 11:14), for the main discussion in the Gemara is when there were threads of tekhelet. But in the view of many, even when there is no tekhelet one is particular about the number of knots and about the windings in each ḥulya, that they be between seven and thirteen, as explained above. The Ra'avad explained that what Rabbi said, \"enough that he winds, repeats and does it a third time\", means that at the least one makes three windings of tekhelet alternating between four windings of white, which in all are seven. Thus far. And so wrote the Shulḥan Arukh (11:14), that in the first ḥulya one makes seven windings, as explained above. And for the second ḥulya he wrote to make 9 windings, but the custom, following the Ari and the Shela, is to make 8, so that the total of the ḥulyot will be the number of the Name 'Havaya' and 'eḥad' (Mishna Berura 11:70; Ben Ish Ḥai, Lekh Lekha 1). Some of those of Sephardi origin are accustomed to wind on a 'tallit katan' according to the Name Havaya, that is, first ḥulya 10, second 5, third 6, fourth 5, as brought in the Magen Avraham (11:22) and Kaf Ha-Ḥayim of Palagi (10:4). And so are accustomed those of Moroccan and Tunisian origin (Alei Hadas 1:25; Rav Messas's introduction to the Mishna Berura with Ish Matzli'aḥ; and so Rav Eliyahu was accustomed). According to the Rambam (Tzitzit 1:7-8), the gedil may consist of seven up to thirteen ḥulyot, in each of which there are three windings, and as explained above, the widespread custom of those of Yemenite origin is to make seven ḥulyot, and some are accustomed to make thirteen ḥulyot, which comes to a total of 39 windings.<br>The form of the winding: the custom of those of Ashkenazi origin is that each winding is one loop of the long thread around its fellows, and the gedil holds by the double knot at the end of the ḥulya. Many of those of Sephardi origin are accustomed at the end of each and every winding to loop the shamash thread under itself in order to strengthen the gedil (Kitzur Shulḥan Arukh of Rav Eliyahu 9:5). And those who follow the Rambam make the tying by looping the shamash under the windings at the end of each ḥulya. (Below in note 17 it is explained how the ḥulyot are wound when there is tekhelet.)<br>Some say that when one needs to cut the tzitzit threads, it is good to cut them with the teeth and not with a metal knife, like the altar, onto which no iron is brought up (the Shela; Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav 11:24; Ben Ish Ḥai, Lekh Lekha 4; Mishna Berura 11:61). But this enhancement is not mentioned among the Rishonim, and likewise many poskim did not write it, which implies that this was not the custom. The Beit Barukh (11:121) wrote that the enhancement is specifically when by the cutting one makes the threads valid, but to shorten them is permitted even for the meticulous. And the enhancement too is only when possible, but when the tzitzit is strong one should not be stringent about this."}]},{"slug":"21-02-12","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-12/","title_he":"יב – ציצית שחוטיה נקרעו","title_en":"12. Tzitzit Whose Threads Have Torn","body_en":"<p>If one of the threads was cut at the place where the gedil joins the garment, the tzitzit are invalid, since the cut thread has become entirely invalid (Mishna Berura 12:13).</p>\n<p>If, after the tzitzit were tied on the garment properly, all the threads coming out of the gedil tore and there remained of them the measure 'enough to make a loop', that is, the measure with which one can loop together all the threads that were cut, which is at most 3.8 cm, the tzitzit are still valid.</p>\n<p>If one thread tore up to the gedil, it is valid. But if two threads tore up to the gedil, it is invalid, lest these two threads belong to one thread, so that of that thread whose two ends were cut there does not remain even 'enough to make a loop' (Shulḥan Arukh 12:1-3).</p>\n<p>But if they took care to tie the threads in such a way that the four threads coming out of one side are always tied together opposite the four threads coming out of the other side, then even if all four on one side tore up to the gedil, and on the other side there remained of the threads only the measure 'enough to make a loop', the tzitzit are valid, since of each of the four threads there remained the measure 'enough to make a loop'.<sup>16</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":16,"en":"'Enough to make a loop' means enough to loop together only the threads that tore (Shulḥan Arukh 12:1; Bi'ur Halakha, s.v. 'Im'). Even when the threads are thick, so that in fact the torn thread cannot loop them, if according to the measure of thin tzitzit threads they could be looped, they are valid (Shulḥan Arukh 12:2). It is agreed by all that 'enough to make a loop' of all eight threads is not more than two fingers, 3.8 cm. And if fewer threads were cut, the measure for looping the cut threads alone is enough. And some are lenient to always estimate 'enough to make a loop' of one thread (the Rif and the Rambam, see Bi'ur Halakha 12:1, s.v. 'Im'), and some are lenient that if 'enough to make a loop' remained in the gedil it is valid, even though no branch remained at all (the Ri); but the custom is to be stringent according to Rashi, that 'enough to make a loop' is from the threads without the gedil (Shulḥan Arukh 12:3).<br>What is explained above, that 'enough to make a loop' must remain of each of the four threads, is according to the view of the Rosh. But according to Rabbeinu Tam, two of the four threads must remain whole on both their sides (and their length about 16 cm, as explained above in note 14). And only of the remaining two may it be that of each thread 'enough to make a loop' remains on one side and on its other side it is cut entirely up to the gedil. In practice one blesses even over tzitzit that became invalid according to the view of Rabbeinu Tam, but ideally it is good to replace the threads in order to satisfy his view too (Shulḥan Arukh 12:1).<br>According to the Taz (12:3), one may join threads that tore by a knot and thereby lengthen them to the required measure, both before putting the tzitzit on the garment and after they were put on and began to become invalid, so that they will not become entirely invalid if more threads tear. But if the tzitzit became invalid, lengthening the threads by tying will not help, because of 'You shall make, and not from what is already made'. And so ruled most of the poskim, among them: Eliya Rabba, Peri Megadim, Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav, Derekh Ha-Ḥayim, Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 12, Mishna Berura 7. However, some say that tying does not help to create a thread (Maharsham 8:9), and some say that only after the thread has torn and become shorter than it needs to be does tying it not help (Beit Yitzḥak, Oraḥ Ḥayim 3). And ideally it is good to take their view into account for the sake of the beauty of the tzitzit."}]},{"slug":"21-02-13","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-13/","title_he":"יג – מצוות התכלת","title_en":"13. The Mitzva of Tekhelet","body_en":"<p>The mitzva of tzitzit is that the tzitzit threads be of white and of tekhelet, as it is said (Bamidbar 15:38): \"that they shall make for themselves tzitzit (of white threads) on the corners of their garments throughout their generations, and they shall put on the tzitzit of the corner a thread of tekhelet.\" The white threads are white threads because they are not dyed, and the tekhelet threads are wool threads dyed with tekhelet. The Sages said (Menaḥot 44a) that the tekhelet was produced from a ḥilazon whose \"body resembles the sea, and whose form resembles a fish, and it comes up once in seventy years, and with its blood tekhelet is dyed; therefore its price is high\". The Sages said that after the fact \"the tekhelet does not hold back the white, and the white does not hold back the tekhelet\" (Menaḥot 38a). Therefore, if he tied only four white threads or only four tekhelet threads, he has fulfilled the mitzva.</p>\n<p>Although the Torah forbade sha'atnez, that is, a garment combining wool and linen, in tzitzit it permitted this. For all the dyed threads were of wool, because wool takes the dye well; so it follows that if the garment was of linen, it is a mitzva to put on it tekhelet threads of wool. The Sages learned this from the juxtaposition of the verses, as it is said (Devarim 22:11-12): \"You shall not wear sha'atnez, wool and linen together. You shall make for yourself twisted threads (tzitzit) on the four corners of your covering with which you cover yourself.\" That is, although sha'atnez is forbidden, for the sake of the mitzva of tzitzit it is permitted (Yevamot 4a-b). But in practice the Sages decreed not to put tekhelet threads of wool on a linen garment, lest people put tzitzit on a 'night garment', which is exempt from tzitzit, and transgress the prohibition of sha'atnez (Menaḥot 40a-b; above halakha 5, note 7).</p>\n<p>The colour white expresses kindness and the supreme Divine conduct, whereas the colour tekhelet expresses the judgement revealed in the world. Tekhelet is a term of destruction (kilayon), as the Sages said (Sifrei, Parashat Shelaḥ 115): \"Why is it called tekhelet? Because the Egyptians were destroyed (nitkalu) in their firstborn… Another explanation: because the Egyptians perished (kalu) in the sea.\" Therefore in the passage of tzitzit the exodus from Egypt is mentioned, for in the exodus from Egypt God's conduct in the world was revealed: that He oversees His world and punishes the sinners.</p>\n<p>The colour tekhelet also resembles the sea, through which God's conduct in the world was revealed in the clearest way, for Israel passed through it and were saved, and the Egyptians drowned and were destroyed. And so the Sages said: \"Why is tekhelet different from all the other colours? Because tekhelet resembles the sea, and the sea resembles the sky, and the sky resembles the sapphire stone, and the sapphire stone resembles the Throne of Glory, as it is said (Shemot 24:10): 'And they saw the God of Israel, and under His feet was as it were a work of sapphire brick, and like the very heavens for clearness', and it is said (Yeḥezkel 1:26): 'like the appearance of a sapphire stone, the likeness of a throne'\" (Menaḥot 43b; Ḥullin 89a). That is, by way of contemplating the sea one can rise and contemplate God's conduct in the world.</p>\n<p>A further hint in the word 'tekhelet' is takhlit (purpose), as they said in the Zohar (III 175b): \"Tekhelet – the purpose of everything\", and therefore tekhelet corresponds to the sefira of 'Malkhut', in which all the hues of the ten sefirot are included. And consequently through it God's conduct in the world is revealed, in punishing the sinners and rewarding the righteous.</p>\n<p>The tzitzit is made of four threads that are doubled into eight. Some say that of the four threads two must be white and two tekhelet (Rashi and Tosafot). And some say three white and one tekhelet (the Ra'avad). And some say that the role of the tekhelet is to wind around the gedil, and for this half a thread is needed, that is, one of eight (the Rambam).</p>\n<p>The Sages said (Menaḥot 39a) that one must begin to wind the windings with a white thread, and afterwards combine windings of tekhelet, and finish the windings with white, because the white is holier, and \"one goes up in holiness and does not go down\".<sup>17</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":17,"en":"<b>The number of tekhelet threads</b>: many of the Rishonim wrote that the four tzitzit threads are divided into two white and two tekhelet, for it stands to reason that they are equal. So wrote Rashi (Menaḥot 38a, s.v. 'Ha-tekhelet'), Tosafot (there, s.v. 'Ha-tekhelet'), the Rosh (Tzitzit 6), the Rivevan, the Rid, R. Shimshon of Sens, the Ittur, the Semak, the Yere'im, the Nimukei Yosef. And so wrote many poskim plainly, among them: the Tur (Oraḥ Ḥayim 11), the Levush (11:4), Olat Tamid (9:5), Shulḥan Arukh Ha-Rav (11:1), Mishna Berura (9:7; 14).<br>In contrast, according to the Rambam (Tzitzit 1:6; Responsa 286) and the Ra'avad (there), one should put one 'thread of tekhelet', according to the plain wording of the Torah. According to the Rambam the meaning is half a thread, which is one of eight strands, with which one winds around the white threads. And so wrote his son Rabbi Avraham, Sefer Ha-Manhig (642), R. Peraḥya (Shabbat 25b), Sefer Ha-Kana, Maggid Meisharim (Parashat Shelaḥ), Sha'ar Ha-Kavanot (Derush 4 on the subject of tzitzit). And Mishkenot Yaakov 13 leaned to this, and so wrote the Or Same'aḥ (Tzitzit 1:6). And according to the Ra'avad one must put one thread of tekhelet and three white, and so wrote the Arukh (s.v. 'Tekhelet'), the Meiri (Yevamot 5b) and the Riaz (Tzitzit 223). And so wrote the Gra (Yahel Or on the Zohar, Pinḥas, and in his glosses on the Sifrei, Parashat Ki Tetze) and the Ḥida (Ḥomat Anakh, Shelaḥ 10). And some wrote 'a thread' without explaining whether they meant as the Rambam or as the Ra'avad, among them: Rav Shmuel bar Ḥofni Gaon, the Ramban and Rabbeinu Baḥya (Bamidbar 15:38). And likewise in the Zohar (III 175b).<br>Rashi wrote (Menaḥot 38a, s.v. 'Ha-tekhelet') that if he made all the threads tekhelet, he has fulfilled the mitzva of tekhelet but not the mitzva of white. And so it emerges from the words of Tosafot (Menaḥot 41b, s.v. 'Ve-im hevi'). And the Rambam (Tzitzit 1:4) did not mention this law. Some say this is because in his view he has lost the whole mitzva (Mishkenot Yaakov, Oraḥ Ḥayim 18; Ha'amek She'ala, She'ilta 127:11; Arukh Ha-Shulḥan 9:3). And some say that his view too is like Rashi's (Radbaz 5:90; Mayim Ḥayim on the Rambam 2:8; Yad Ha-Ketana 1:4).<br>Following this discussion the question arose what the law is according to the Rambam and the Ra'avad if he put two threads of tekhelet and two of white. Some say that he did not fulfil the mitzva of white, and perhaps did not fulfil the mitzva of tzitzit at all (Rav Rapman, 'Ve-haya lakhem le-tzitzit' 4). And some say that he fulfilled the mitzva of white and of tekhelet, but did not act properly (Magen Giborim 11:20). In any case it stands to reason that even according to the Rambam, one who acts as the Ra'avad will not be considered as having neglected the mitzva of white (see Sifrei Shelaḥ 115). Therefore there is room to prefer acting as the Ra'avad, for this is the deciding view between the two views, and the Gra and the Ḥida wrote according to it. And so it stands to reason more, that they dyed a whole thread with tekhelet, as emerges from Eruvin 96b, regarding the law of one who finds a thread of tekhelet, where they debated how they would know that it was made for the sake of tzitzit, and they did not answer that one could know this from only half of it being dyed.<br><b>The form of the windings</b> (see above note 15 regarding white threads): there are three main explanations of the custom of the windings of white and tekhelet, and from them further explanations branched out. <b>(a)</b> According to Tosafot (Menaḥot 39a, s.v. 'Lo yifḥot') and the Rosh (15), a ḥulya is three windings of the colour tekhelet or white, and one makes seven ḥulyot alternately. After the first knot, a ḥulya of white and a ḥulya of tekhelet and a knot, and so twice more, and after the fourth knot one makes a ḥulya of white only and a knot. So there are seven ḥulyot and five knots. And some combine thirteen ḥulyot of white and tekhelet alternately between the five knots (the Gra). <b>(b)</b> According to the Ra'avad (Tzitzit 1:7) following Rav Natronai Gaon, Rabbeinu Gershom (Moshav Zekenim, Bamidbar 15:38) and the Nimukei Yosef (Tzitzit 12b), between the five knots there are four ḥulyot, and in each ḥulya there must be between seven and thirteen windings. The windings are made alternately of a white thread and a tekhelet thread, with the first and last winding in each ḥulya of white, except for the second ḥulya, about which one is not particular. <b>(c)</b> According to the Rambam (there), one does not make five knots, since they were not mentioned in the Gemara. According to his view, a ḥulya is three consecutive windings, with a space and a tying between one ḥulya and the next by looping the winding thread under the windings at the end of each ḥulya. All the windings are made of tekhelet except the first and the last, which are of white. And so wrote the Ba'al Ha-Arukh (s.v. 'Tekhelet'). Some added to his view five knots, within which the ḥulyot are combined (the Ḥinukh 386; Ben Ish Ḥai, Lekh Lekha 1). As said above, all the windings are valid. However, for those who wind according to the view of the Ra'avad and his school there is a virtue in that they continue to wind as they were accustomed with white, for the Beit Yosef explained (11:14) that the custom of the windings follows the view of the Ra'avad."}]},{"slug":"21-02-14","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-14/","title_he":"יד – ביטול התכלת והחיפוש אחריה","title_en":"14. The Loss of the Tekhelet and the Search for It","body_en":"<p>The tekhelet was produced on the northern shores of the Land from certain snails that live in the sea. When the Jewish settlement in the Land dwindled, it became hard to obtain tekhelet. In the days of the Talmud there were still those who fulfilled the mitzva of tekhelet, but in the days of the Geonim they could no longer obtain the tekhelet dye, and they would tie the tzitzit with white threads only.<sup>18</sup></p>\n<p>Apparently several factors led to the loss of the tekhelet from Israel. First, there were periods in which the government issued decrees against the production of tekhelet. Second, following Byzantine rule and the Arab conquest, the Jewish settlement in the Land kept dwindling until there were no longer Jews who would engage in producing the tekhelet. And they could not have it produced by gentiles, because the dyeing of the wool with tekhelet must be done 'for its own sake'. Third, following the Arab conquest, the Land of Israel and its surroundings gradually sank into poverty, and there were no longer many among the peoples who sought the expensive tekhelet dye. So the snails and the way tekhelet was produced from them were forgotten for more than a thousand years.</p>\n<p>One can perhaps say that when Israel dwelt in their Land and the Temple stood in its place and the Divine Presence rested in Israel, God's conduct was revealed in the world, doing good to the righteous and punishing the wicked, and this found expression in the thread of tekhelet in the tzitzit, which expresses judgement. But after the Temple was destroyed and Israel were exiled from their land, God's conduct is not revealed in this world, and the tekhelet was lost. But in the eternal world, the value of faith and the mitzvot remains in its place, and God in His great mercy and kindness oversees His people in hiding, to bring them forward towards their redemption; and correspondingly Israel fulfil the mitzva of tzitzit with white threads only. And as Israel return to their Land and the Divine Presence returns to dwell among them, they will return to combining threads of tekhelet in the tzitzit.</p>\n<p>About a hundred and eighty years ago rabbis and scientists began to search for the tekhelet. In the year 5647 the Rebbe of Radzin, Rav Gershon Ḥanokh Leiner, began to spread his position that the tekhelet was produced from the fish 'common cuttlefish', and in 5648 he began to dye threads of tekhelet from it, and his ḥasidim used to wear them. But there are many difficulties with his identification: first, the cuttlefish is very different from the Sages' description of the ḥilazon. Second, its colour tends to black and not blue, and only with the help of chemical components added to the cuttlefish's blood does it become blue, and these components can dye other materials blue too. Third, this colour fades naturally or through laundering, unlike the tekhelet, whose colour according to tradition was fast.</p>\n<p>Rav Herzog held that the tekhelet was produced from the 'Janthina' snail, but his proposal too is difficult, because the colour of the 'Janthina' is not absorbed well by wool. In addition, no archaeological or historical support has been found for producing tekhelet from it.<sup>19</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":18,"en":"Although one who puts white without tekhelet has fulfilled the mitzva of white, one may ask about the custom of Israel to fulfil the mitzva with white only: since there is no mitzva to wear a square garment that is obliged in tzitzit, perhaps it is preferable not to wear a garment obliged in tzitzit, and consequently not to neglect the mitzva of tekhelet? Some answer that the mitzva of white is the main one. In addition, from what is said, 'throughout their generations', we learned that one must fulfil the mitzva even when there is no tekhelet (Rav Perla's commentary on Rav Sa'adya, positive commandments 7). And the Sha'agat Arye (32) wrote that when one is prevented from fulfilling the mitzva there is no neglect of a positive commandment, and therefore one fulfils the mitzva with white."},{"n":19,"en":"<b>The way the tekhelet is produced</b>: the Torah does not say from what the tekhelet must be produced, and the Sages explained that its source is the blood of the ḥilazon (Menaḥot 44a), and they forbade tekhelet dye from 'indigo' (Bava Metzia 61b). Many hold that only from this ḥilazon is it permitted to produce tekhelet. So wrote Rashi (there, s.v. 'Kala ilan'), Tosafot (Menaḥot 42a, s.v. 'Ve-samemanim'), the Ḥinukh 386, R. Avraham son of the Rambam (on Shemot 25:4) and others. And some understood the Rambam so (Tzitzit 2:1-2), among them: R. Yitzḥak Tayeb (Vavei Ha-Amudim on the Yere'im 401), Avnei Nezer (Oraḥ Ḥayim 15:8-9), Reshit Bikkurim (2:2). On the other hand, according to the Tiferet Yisrael (introduction to Seder Mo'ed, chapter 3), the main thing is that the colour be blue and fast, and it does not matter what its source is, and the problem with 'indigo' is that its colour fades. And so many understand the Rambam, and Rav Herzog leaned to this view (Ha-Tekhelet Be-Yisrael, chapter 4).<br>According to the Rambam (Tzitzit 2:2) it is permitted, at the time of dyeing the tekhelet, to add further ingredients to the blood of the ḥilazon, and according to Tosafot (Menaḥot 42b, s.v. 'Ve-samemanim') it is forbidden to add; however, before the dyeing it is permitted to soak the wool in further ingredients."}]},{"slug":"21-02-15","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-15/","title_he":"טו – הזיהוי כ'ארגמון קהה קוצים'","title_en":"15. The Identification as the Banded Dye-Murex","body_en":"<p>In recent decades evidence has begun to accumulate that the tekhelet dye was produced from a snail that lives inside a shell, called the 'banded dye-murex' (argamon kehe kotzim), which was used in the past to produce argaman dye (reddish-purple), and which, by being exposed to the sun in the process of preparing the dye, turns blue. First, its features fit in general the Sages' description (Menaḥot 44a) of the ḥilazon from which they produced the tekhelet. 'Its body resembles the sea', for tiny creatures accumulate on its shell and give it a bluish-grey appearance. 'Its form resembles a fish', in that it lives in the sea. 'It comes up once in seventy years', in that it is hard to obtain, and once every several years a special climatic phenomenon occurs that causes many of them to float on the surface of the sea. 'With its blood tekhelet is dyed': from a fluid present in its body tekhelet is produced. The Sages further said that its blood is taken while it is alive (Shabbat 75a), and indeed if time passes after its death no dye can be produced from it. Second, in the area between Haifa and Tyre, where the Sages said the tekhelet was produced, many archaeological remains have been found of dye production in large quantities from the 'banded dye-murex'. And it stands to reason that it was the royal dye used in ancient times to produce argaman and blue dyes, and it is this that the Torah commanded to put in the tzitzit. Further, since we do not find that the Sages warned against forgery except from 'kala ilan' (indigo), it implies that there was no other ḥilazon from which tekhelet could be produced.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, some hold that the 'banded dye-murex' is not the ḥilazon from which tekhelet was produced. First, its main colour is argaman and not blue, and it was used for this throughout the Roman and Byzantine empires, and only by exposure to the sun with the addition of a chemical substance that helps the dye to be absorbed can one dye effectively in blue. Second, it does not match the Sages' precise description. Its body does not resemble the sea; rather, its colour is translucent grey like a snail. It is not a fish but a mollusc with a shell. It does not come up once in seventy years but can always be found. One does not dye with its blood but with a fluid extracted from a gland in its body.</p>\n<p>In addition, it appears that they continued to produce dye from the 'banded dye-murex' also in the period of the Rishonim, after the Geonim and the Rishonim wrote that the tekhelet had been lost to us; and from the fact that they did not produce tekhelet for tzitzit it is implied that this snail is not the ḥilazon from which they used to produce tekhelet.<sup>20</sup></p>\n<p>In addition, there is a doubt what the colour of the tekhelet is. Many hold that it is dark blue, but some hold that it is light blue, turquoise or even purple. And consequently there is a doubt whether the colour that comes from the 'banded dye-murex', which is dark blue, is the correct colour according to halakha.<sup>21</sup></p>","notes_en":[{"n":20,"en":"Some say that a great deal of evidence has accumulated around the 'banded dye-murex', and many rabbis and researchers share in identifying it as the ḥilazon from which the tekhelet was produced, to the point that one can determine with certainty that the tekhelet was produced from it (Dr. Ziderman, Teḥumin 9; and so hold some of the rabbis who encourage tekhelet today). On the other hand, some say that there is great doubt whether the tekhelet was produced from the 'banded dye-murex', and it may be that in the future it will become clear that tekhelet was produced in another way. It is a fact that in the time of the Rebbe of Radzin too, the supporters of his position were confident that tekhelet was produced from the cuttlefish, and today it is fairly clear that it was not as he said. Therefore, as long as there is no complete certainty the matter remains in doubt (Rav Aviner, Iturei Kohanim 140; Minḥat Asher 2:2-4; Rav Yehoshua Inbal, Ha-Otzar 11). And indeed, according to what we know now, the likelihood is that this is the tekhelet, but the matter is not decided (so wrote Prof. Zohar Amar in the book 'Ha-Argaman', chapter 5, published by Machon Har Bracha). And one should not compare the identification of the murex to the identification of the cuttlefish, since the identification of the murex rests on many rabbis and researchers who have held to their position for decades, unlike the identification of the Rebbe of Radzin.<br>However, there is a difficulty: if the tekhelet was produced from the 'banded dye-murex', why was the tekhelet forgotten? After all, this snail was known throughout the period of forgetting, and was even used for dyeing in the Byzantine Empire for hundreds of years after the disappearance of the tekhelet from Israel. The traveller Benjamin of Tudela too reported that there were Jews in important communities in Greece and Turkey who made their living from making fine garments of argaman, \"and among them great scholars in Mishna and Talmud\" (The Travels of Benjamin of Tudela). Some answered that perhaps they produced the argaman in another way and not from this snail, which was indeed forgotten (see 'Ve-haya lakhem le-tzitzit' 1). But it is hard to accept this explanation, since according to the findings in our hands it appears that in all generations they continued to produce argaman dye from the 'banded dye-murex'. And perhaps they forgot how to produce tekhelet from it. But it is still difficult how expert dyers could forget such a thing. And perhaps there were generations in which, because of decrees or poverty, they stopped producing tekhelet, and afterwards, when they returned to dyeing, they had forgotten the tradition of producing the tekhelet. And perhaps the price of the tekhelet was very high, until the rabbis ruled not to try to obtain tekhelet so that people would not, because of it, neglect fulfilling the mitzva with white, and perhaps for this reason the way of producing the tekhelet was entirely forgotten (and perhaps this can be understood from Yeshu'ot Malko, Oraḥ Ḥayim 2)."},{"n":21,"en":"The Sages said (Menaḥot 43b) that the colour of the tekhelet resembles the sea, which resembles the sky. And in the Yerushalmi (Berakhot 1:2) they said that the tekhelet resembles the sea, which resembles the grasses, which resemble the sky. In fact, rabbis and researchers have been in doubt about what the colour of the tekhelet in the Torah is. Many hold that it is dark blue, for the Sages said (Bava Metzia 61b) that the tekhelet could be forged with 'kala ilan', and the Arukh wrote that this is what is called 'indigo', from which a dark blue dye was produced, and it resembles the sky at evening ('Ptil Tekhelet' 2; 'Ha-Tekhelet Be-Yisrael' 6). And some say that it is light blue, like the midday sky, which in our language is called tekhelet (R. Avraham son of the Rambam on Shemot 25:4; Tiferet Yisrael). And some say that it is purple (Mekor Ḥayim 18:3; Dr. Ziderman, Teḥumin 9; and Prof. Koren, Teḥumin 44). And some say that it is turquoise (R. Shlomo Sirilio on Yerushalmi Berakhot 1:2; Prof. Felix in 'Ḥai Ve-Tzome'aḥ Ba-Torah', pp. 94-95). See the summary of the views in the book 'Ha-Tekhelet' (pp. 299-304, by my friend Rav Menaḥem Burstein). After all this, the prevalent view is that the tekhelet of the Torah is the colour blue, and it appears that they produced it in different shades, for they held that all shades of blue are valid (Ptil Tekhelet 2, by the Rebbe of Radzin; and so emerges from Responsa Radbaz 5:48; Rav Rak, Teḥumin 16; Ḥotam Shel Zahav 26-27). In general, the blue colour that comes from the 'banded dye-murex' through exposure to the sun is dark blue, but a wide range of colours can be produced from it, such as argaman, blue tending to purple, turquoise and more."}]},{"slug":"21-02-16","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-16/","title_he":"טז – הוויכוח להלכה","title_en":"16. The Halakhic Debate","body_en":"<p>Some hold that it has already become clear that the tekhelet was produced from the 'banded dye-murex', and therefore it is obligatory to dye tekhelet threads for tzitzit from it. And even for those who argue that the identification is not absolute, we must act according to the best data in our hands, and therefore it is obligatory to put in the tzitzit tekhelet from the 'banded dye-murex'. In addition, since this is a mitzva from the Torah, in a situation of doubt one must be stringent. And even if it turns out that the identification is mistaken, since there is no prohibition against dyeing the white threads in various colours, at most these tekhelet threads will be considered white threads that were dyed in various colours. In addition, some hold that the mitzva is not to put tekhelet that comes specifically from the blood of a particular ḥilazon, but the main thing is that it be a fast tekhelet colour; and indeed in the past the only way to produce it was from a particular ḥilazon, but by law any fast blue dye is valid for the tekhelet of the tzitzit (Tiferet Yisrael, see above note 19).</p>\n<p>On the other hand, some hold that there are great doubts about the identification of the tekhelet as produced from the 'banded dye-murex', and therefore there is no mitzva to put it in the tzitzit. Moreover, many hold that without a tradition about the tekhelet and the ways of examining it, even if it seems that in all likelihood the tekhelet has been found, there is no possibility of obligating its use. Nor is this considered a Torah doubt in which one must be stringent, for since for more than a thousand years it has been established in Israel that the tekhelet was lost, without certainty the mitzva cannot be restored to its former state. Therefore, only when we know with certainty what the tekhelet is, or alternatively when the Sanhedrin decides that this is how the mitzva must be fulfilled, will there be an obligation to put tekhelet in the tzitzit.<sup>22</sup></p>\n<p>In addition, following the loss of the tradition of putting tekhelet in the tzitzit, there are disputes and doubts that we have no possibility of deciding: (a) how many tekhelet threads one must put in the tzitzit (above note 17); (b) whether it is permitted, when producing the tekhelet, to add further substances to the blood of the ḥilazon (above note 19); (c) what the shade of the tekhelet is – dark blue, light blue, turquoise or even purple (above note 21). And as long as there is no clear decision on these issues, there is no possibility of claiming that it is obligatory to put tekhelet in the tzitzit.</p>\n<p>And since in their view there is no obligation to put tekhelet, many of them hold that one should refrain from it, for one must take care not to implant in the Jewish people a doubtful identification of a mitzva. They further argued that those who go with tekhelet appear to be acting arrogantly towards the elder rabbis who do not do so. They further argued that one should take into account the view of those who hold that ideally the colour of the threads should be like the colour of the garment, and it is forbidden to dye them in other colours (above in note 8). Further, it may be that if many go with tekhelet, among the broad public that is used to going with white threads only, as is the prevalent custom, there will be people who think that it has no value and will become weaker in fulfilling the mitzva of tzitzit. Likewise, there is a concern that because of the high price of the tekhelet, if people hold that it is obligatory to put tekhelet, there will be those who prefer to wear a four-cornered garment less, especially children and youths whose tzitzit sometimes tear. And it is better that everyone always fulfil the mitzva with white than that they sometimes enhance it with tekhelet.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":22,"en":"Those who obligate putting tekhelet hold that even if there is a doubt about its identification, since this is a mitzva from the Torah one must be stringent, according to the law of 'a Torah doubt is treated stringently'. In addition, it emerges from the words of the Sha'agat Arye (as brought above in note 18) that only when there is no tekhelet is it preferable to go with white alone, but when there is tekhelet, since one fulfils the mitzva with white only after the fact, it is preferable to go without a four-cornered garment than to put white alone and neglect the positive commandment of tekhelet (the Rebbe of Radzin, 'Ptil Tekhelet' 5).<br>On the other hand, those who hold that one should not put tekhelet answer that the whole fulfilment of the mitzvot is according to tradition, and since the tradition about the tekhelet was discontinued, it cannot be renewed (Rav Y. D. Soloveitchik, 'Nefesh Ha-Rav', pp. 53-54). And it appears that even according to this view, if the matter is proven with certainty, the mitzva will return to its place without the need for a tradition. They further argued that when a doubt stems from a lack of knowledge, it is not considered a 'Torah doubt' (Minḥat Asher 2:2, based on the Rashba and the Beit Yosef, Yoreh De'ah 190:31). And some argue that one is stringent in a Torah doubt only where, if one is stringent, one will certainly fulfil the mitzva, which is not the case here (Peri Megadim, Oraḥ Ḥayim, Eshel Avraham 194:3). And the answer of those who obligate putting tekhelet is that a doubt of knowledge is not considered a doubt only for stringency, but not for leniency. And they also brought proofs from various places that there is an obligation to fulfil mitzvot because of a doubt even when it is not clear that one will fulfil them (the Rebbe of Radzin, there). However, these answers were not accepted by those who hold that one should not put tekhelet, since the basic situation is that the tekhelet was forgotten from Israel, to the point that the situation in which we have no tekhelet is considered a kind of ḥazaka (established presumption), and in order to uproot it and found the tradition of the tekhelet anew, absolute arguments with no doubt in them are needed, as we find in many places in halakha that one follows a ḥazaka as long as it has not been uprooted. Something similar was written by Rav Yehoshua of Kutno (Yeshu'ot Malko, Oraḥ Ḥayim 2-3)."}]},{"slug":"21-02-17","link":"https://ph.yhb.org.il/21-02-17/","title_he":"יז – סיכום למעשה","title_en":"17. Summary in Practice","body_en":"<p>According to what appears today, the likelihood is that the tekhelet dye of the tzitzit was produced from the 'banded dye-murex'; but since there is no certainty in this, without an agreed ruling of the majority in number and the majority in wisdom of the Sages of Israel, there is no obligation to include tekhelet threads in the tzitzit. This is because the Torah is fulfilled according to the instruction of the Sages of Israel, and when the tradition of the tekhelet was discontinued, it cannot be restored as an obligation or even as a 'doubtful obligation' without the decision of the Sanhedrin or the agreement of the majority in number and the majority in wisdom of the Sages of Israel.<sup>23</sup></p>\n<p>However, since it stands to reason that this is the tekhelet in which our forefathers went, there is a virtue in putting tekhelet threads in the tzitzit. And one need not be concerned about arrogance, since tens of thousands already do so. However, one whose father or rabbi insists that he not go with tekhelet had better listen to him and thereby fulfil the Torah's mitzva of honouring him, than enhance the mitzva by putting tekhelet.</p>\n<p>In addition, it is right for one who enhances the mitzva by putting tekhelet to emphasise that he does so without a vow, and it is even fitting that he sometimes wrap himself in a tallit of white. In this way he will not create a new obligation without the instruction of the Sages as a whole. And in this way the concern that there will be people who become weaker in fulfilling the mitzva also lessens, since whenever it is hard to put tekhelet, they will willingly fulfil the mitzva with white. For example, children and youths whose tzitzit tend to tear will be able to go with white even ideally. Likewise, soldiers who need to change their 'tallit katan' frequently will be able to go with white even ideally.</p>\n<p>If no challenge arises to the way of producing the tekhelet from the 'banded dye-murex', the custom of going with this tekhelet will keep spreading until it becomes a custom that it is right to keep. And if people continue to strengthen it, it will become a binding custom in many communities, until gradually, it may be that even without a decision of the Sanhedrin, by the 'acceptance of the nation' and its Sages, the Torah's mitzva will return to being fulfilled with this tekhelet, and it will be obligatory from the Torah to include it in the tzitzit.</p>","notes_en":[{"n":23,"en":"The tradition of the Torah is entrusted to the Sages, as it is said (Devarim 17:8-11): \"If a matter of judgement is too hard for you… then you shall arise and go up to the place which the Lord your God shall choose. And you shall come to the priests the Levites, and to the judge that shall be in those days, and you shall inquire, and they shall tell you the sentence of judgement. And you shall do according to the sentence which they shall tell you from that place which the Lord shall choose, and you shall observe to do according to all that they instruct you. According to the Torah which they shall instruct you, and according to the judgement which they shall tell you, you shall do; you shall not turn aside from the sentence which they shall tell you, to the right or to the left.\" However, this absolute authority was given to the Great Court that sat in the Chamber of Hewn Stone, whose Sages were ordained one from another back to Moshe our teacher. But even afterwards, when in a gradual process the authority of the Sages weakened, up to the abolition of the Great Court, the abolition of ordination and the dispersion of the exiles, the Sages of Israel still carry out their mission in continuing the tradition of the Torah as required for the fulfilment of the Torah and the mitzvot. And in place of the organised court, the Sages accepted by Israel discuss the issues, and over the years and generations the halakha is determined. Therefore, as long as it has not been agreed by most of the Sages of Israel that it is obligatory to put tekhelet, there is no obligation in this (see the Ḥinukh 496, and Li-Nevukhei Ha-Dor 6:1. And likewise regarding 'we act as their agents', Gittin 88b; Bava Kamma 84b; Shulḥan Arukh, Ḥoshen Mishpat 1:1; Rambam, Laws of the Sanhedrin 4:11)."}]}]});
